Title: Senior/Lead Backend Exercise - Real-time A/V over WebSockets

Goal
You will design and implement a backend system for real-time audio/video
communication where media flows strictly over WebSockets (no WebRTC/UDP). The
system must handle high fanout, low latency, and reliability, with metadata and
audit stored in Postgres. Expectation is at least 2 days of work to design and
implement properly.

Tech constraints (must use)
- Python (3.10+ recommended)
- WebSocket-based connections for all real-time traffic
- Postgres as the primary database

Optional tech (design only)
- You may propose caches, queues, or stream processors in your design, but the
  implementation must still work with only Python + Postgres.

What we are evaluating (weighted)
- System design (40%)
- Coding skills (40%)
- Low-level design (20%)

Problem statement
Build the backend for a real-time audio/video communication service where:
- Users join rooms and publish/subscribe to A/V streams over WebSockets.
- The server relays media frames to all subscribers in a room.
- The system enforces ordering per stream, handles backpressure, and keeps
  latency low.

Functional requirements
1) Rooms and sessions
   - Create a room with: room_id, title, max_participants.
   - Users can JOIN and LEAVE rooms over WebSockets.
   - Room state transitions: IDLE -> LIVE -> ENDED.

2) Media publish/subscribe
   - Media frames are sent over WebSockets as binary messages.
   - Each publisher has one audio stream and one video stream.
   - Each stream carries a monotonic frame sequence number.
   - The server relays frames to all subscribers in the room.
   - If a client reconnects, it can request a replay gap fill by sequence.

3) Real-time control
   - Clients can mute/unmute, pause/resume video, or switch video quality.
   - Control messages are JSON over WebSockets.
   - The server broadcasts state changes to the room.

4) History and audit
   - Store room/session metadata and participant events in Postgres.
   - Store per-stream stats (bitrate, fps, packet loss estimates).
   - Media payloads do NOT need to be persisted.

5) Reliability and correctness
   - No duplicate delivery per subscriber for the same sequence.
   - Backpressure handling to avoid unbounded memory growth.
   - Graceful handling of disconnects and reconnections.

Non-functional requirements (design for these)
- 100k concurrent WebSocket connections across rooms.
- Hot room: 200 publishers, 50k subscribers (fanout).
- P99 end-to-end latency < 300ms for audio, < 600ms for video.
- 99.95% availability for live rooms.
- Zero loss of room metadata and participant events.

System design deliverable
Provide a design document that includes:
- Capacity estimates and assumptions.
- Architecture diagram (components and data flow).
- Scaling strategy: horizontal scaling, room sharding, and stickiness.
- WebSocket fanout and backpressure strategy.
- Data model and indexing strategy in Postgres.
- Failure modes and recovery strategies.
- Consistency model for stream ordering and replay.
- Observability: metrics, logs, tracing, alerts.
- Tradeoffs and alternatives you considered.

Low-level design deliverable
Provide a document or section that includes:
- Module breakdown and key class/function responsibilities.
- Detailed sequence diagrams for:
  - join_room
  - publish_frame
  - subscribe_and_replay
- WebSocket message protocol (envelope + message types).
- Database schema with constraints and indexes.
- Concurrency control approach (locks, queues, isolation levels).
- Backpressure and drop strategy (audio vs video).

Coding deliverable (required implementation)
Implement a minimal but correct system that runs locally:
- A WebSocket server that supports:
  - AUTH (token or api_key is fine)
  - JOIN {room_id}
  - LEAVE {room_id}
  - PUBLISH {room_id, stream_type, seq, payload}
  - SUBSCRIBE {room_id, stream_type, since_seq}
  - CONTROL {room_id, action, value}
- Atomic persistence of room/session metadata to Postgres.
- Monotonic sequence per stream (no gaps for accepted frames).
- Snapshot + live updates for subscribers.
- Backpressure handling with a bounded buffer.
- Rate limiting per publisher and per room (simple in-memory acceptable).
- Tests:
  - Concurrent publishers in same room (ordering + fanout).
  - Reconnect and replay from last sequence.
  - Backpressure drop policy (video drops first).

Implementation notes (not requirements)
- Framework choice is yours (FastAPI, Starlette, raw websockets, etc).
- You can keep the implementation as a single service, but show how it would
  scale out.
- Keep dependencies minimal.
- Provide a README with setup steps and how to run tests.

Stretch goals (if time permits)
- Dynamic quality adaptation (server-directed).
- Room ownership/partitioning with leader election.
- Per-connection flow control and adaptive buffering.
- Jitter buffer with playout delay control.
- TLS, authn/z, and multi-tenant isolation.

Submission format
- README with setup/run/test instructions.
- Design doc (markdown or pdf).
- Code + tests.
- Any scripts or docker compose you want to include.

Clarifications
- You are not expected to implement a full UI.
- You can stub auth, but must show where it integrates.
- You can mock external services in tests.

What good looks like
- Clear, testable design assumptions.
- Correctness under concurrent publish/subscribe.
- Practical scaling strategy.
- Clean, readable code with tests and good error handling.
